PF AND ZFS DTRACE COVERAGE: TWELFTH EXPANSION
===========================================

21 new contracts across five runtime sources, plus one newly connected path
for the existing zfs:::access contract. Seven base-install profiles. This is
a focused nine-category PF/ZFS expansion, not complete networking/storage or
complete PF/OpenZFS coverage.

History and existing coverage
----------------------------
Reviewed PF source-limiter capacity fix 9e33f72d94e: address-entry exhaustion
must honor PF_LIMITER_BLOCK; explicit fallthrough remains unchanged. The new
sites observe those decisions without altering counters, rule selection,
block reasons, limiter policy or recovery behavior. Existing rule/state,
translation, Dummynet and route probes are retained rather than duplicated.

Existing OpenZFS ARC hit/miss/eviction, ZIL error and TXG lifecycle probes are
retained. New wait boundaries complement them. The FreeBSD access completion
probe existed, but the successful VEXEC shortcut returned before it; that
ordinary VOP access path now emits the same contract. No site is added to the
special SMR fast-lookup permission path.

Consulted /home/koryheard/5bsd-issues/2026-10-03-zfs-lock-order-reversal.md,
including its 2026-10-12 diagnosis: the recorded cycle was fixed by removing
the procdesc/proctree edge in 425f274a444. These changes add no lock acquisition
and are not another fix for that incident or the separate tmpfs report.

Nine-category mapping
---------------------
lifecycle: ZIL waiter allocate/release; DMU assignment start/done
performance: DMU assignment; dirty/dnode waits; TXG wait; ZIL commit waiter; FreeBSD vnode read/write
errors: PF limiter rejection reasons; DMU attempt and terminal errors; TXG EINTR/ESHUTDOWN; ZIL waiter error/issuance timeout; vnode errno
resources: PF states/source entries/rate budget pressure; ZFS dirty-data limit wait; ZIL waiter allocation/release
io: ZFS vnode requested offset/length and completion residual/errno
network: PF state/source limiter decisions and eligibility
synchronization: ZFS dirty-data/dnode/TXG/ZIL waits
access-control: PF block versus fallthrough; existing ZFS access probe connected to successful execute shortcut
counters-state: PF pressure/eligibility events; ZFS dirty bytes and assigned/synced transaction-group progress

PF limiter contracts and scope
------------------------------
pf:ip:limiter:decision(kind, id, rule_number, reason, configured_action, block)
  kind: 0 state limiter, 1 source limiter.
  reason: 0 missing limiter, 1 hard state limit, 2 rate limit,
          3 source-address entry capacity.
  block: 1 for the PF_LIMITER_BLOCK branch, 0 for rule fallthrough.
  Missing limiters preserve existing PF_TEST_ATTRIB fallthrough regardless
  of configured action; the event reports block=0 and never dereferences
  the absent limiter. No firewall policy is changed.
  Seven source sites cover two missing-limiter cases, state hard/rate limits,
  source hard/rate limits and the new source-entry capacity branch.

pf:ip:limiter:pressure(kind, id, reason, observed, threshold, configured_action)
  Five source sites, on the hard/rate/capacity rejection branches only.
  Hard-limit values are states, capacity values are source entries, and rate
  values are elapsed nanoseconds versus the token interval. Samples add no
  locking and should not be treated as an atomic multi-field snapshot beyond
  the original path's synchronization guarantees. Missing limiters have no
  pressure event because there is no object to read.

pf:ip:limiter:eligible(kind, id, rule_number)
  One site after each limiter's checks pass. This is NOT packet admission or
  successful state allocation: later packet-rate checks, rules and other
  stages can still reject the packet. It permits correlating rejection and
  renewed eligibility without inventing a persistent recovery state.

These sites execute in PF's existing rule-test context and may be high
volume. No packet buffers, IP addresses, ports, limiter names or payloads
are copied. The profile groups decisions by numeric identifiers/reasons and
uses pressure histograms rather than an aggregation key for every value.

ZFS contracts and scope
-----------------------
sdt:::dmu-assign-start(tx, flags) / done(tx, assigned_txg, error)
  Enclose assignment attempts and their waits after initial setup. Failure
  uses assigned_txg=0; success uses the actual assigned txg. Both return
  sites are verified. dmu-assign-retry(tx, attempt_error) records a failed
  try_assign before normalization/unassignment; it can precede a terminal
  return, so its name does not promise another attempt. Done reports the
  final ERESTART/EIO/ENOSPC/EDQUOT or success as returned by the original code.

sdt:::dmu-dirty-wait-start(pool, dirty_bytes, limit_bytes)
  / done(pool, remaining_dirty_bytes)
  Surround each actual condition-variable sleep at the dirty-data maximum.
  Observations are under dp_lock; wake completion includes reacquisition.
  This does not cover every throttle/delay path or assert that pressure is
  resolved after one wake. Existing delay-mintime instrumentation remains.

sdt:::dmu-dnode-wait-start(dnode, assigned_txg)
  / done(dnode, assigned_txg)
  Surround each assignment-conflict sleep under dn_mtx. Done is a wake, not
  proof that the next loop test permits assignment.

sdt:::txg-wait-start(pool, target_txg, flags)
  / done(pool, target_txg, synced_txg, error)
  Cover the sync wait after acquiring tx_sync_lock and resolving a zero
  target. Include repeated sleeps and EINTR/ESHUTDOWN; exclude initial mutex
  acquisition. An already-satisfied target produces a short successful pair.

sdt:::zil-wait-start(zilog, waiter) / done(zilog, waiter, error)
  Cover the commit waiter including its initial mutex acquisition. Completion
  reads the final waiter error under the lock. It is not a direct fsync return
  contract: callers can fall back to TXG syncing. zil-wait-timeout(zilog,
  waiter) marks an issuance timeout that invokes the existing timeout helper;
  it is not a storage I/O failure. No potentially freed LWB is dereferenced.
  zil-waiter-allocate(waiter) runs after full initialization; release(waiter)
  runs before cache free and exposes identity only, after private teardown.

sdt:::zfs-vop-read-start / zfs-vop-write-start(vnode, offset, length, flags)
  / done(vnode, residual_length, error)
  Surround the FreeBSD zfs_read/zfs_write calls. Length/residual reveal short
  transfers; errors include their original direct-I/O/user-copy failures.
  These are vnode operation boundaries, not physical device I/O completion
  or stable-storage guarantees. No extra clock reads or payload copies occur.

zfs:::access(object_id, access_mode, credential_pointer, error)
  Existing contract, unchanged argument order. Successful ordinary VEXEC
  shortcut now emits it once with error=0. Other paths still emit the existing
  final result. The supplied profile reads only mode and errno; it does not
  dereference the credential pointer or collect credentials.

