Backend and context safety
--------------------------
Shared OpenZFS sources use trace_bsd.h forwarding macros only in FreeBSD kernel
builds. Other platforms and userland discard the calls and their arguments:
Linux tracing requires separately declared events, so these additions must
not introduce undefined Linux tracepoint calls. The FreeBSD vnode source uses
its existing DTRACE_PROBE convention. No upstream Linux runtime coverage or
cross-platform build validation is claimed; the foreign guard test tests only
that preprocessing branch. Base libzpool's three affected shared sources build.

There are no new sites in early initialization, hard interrupt filters,
NMI/IPI handlers, DTrace internals or allocator internals. The PF sites are
in the normal packet rule path, so enable them selectively. ZFS wait/state
observations use existing object ownership and locks; no new locking is
introduced. Profiles never dereference kernel pointers. Native inactive SDT
patchpoints are NOPs; compiled-out configurations have no new probe symbols.
Enabled tracing still costs time and should not be described as overhead-free.

