The existing bsdinstruments Makefile selects all seven profiles for the base
bsdinstruments package under /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles. The catalog
contains 388 entries. No utility executable, package publication or system
installation is required to review these source changes.

Paired profiles use per-pid/tid depth and timestamps, clear completed entries,
and aggregate elapsed microseconds. Start before workload; lost events can
invalidate pairing. PID filtering selects the current execution context,
not the process owning a packet, pool or asynchronous I/O. Resource allocation
counts are events since tracing started, not a complete live-object census.

Validation and reproduction
---------------------------
Artifact root: /usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-twelfth-validation
Prepared kernel: /usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC-NODEBUG
Prepared world sysroot: /usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/tmp
Runners: coverage-twelfth-{modules,compile,base,guards,codegen,check}.py
All compilation uses the available CPUs (-j16 here). Module-source checks use
the modules' real flags, including OpenZFS's existing per-source suppressions.
An initial raw flag query omitted those suppressions; it was corrected rather
than changing unrelated imported code or lowering the module's warning policy.

  Two native module links: pf.ko and zfs.ko, with new probe-symbol verification.
  Ten source builds: five kernel sources, KDTRACE_HOOKS enabled and disabled.
  Ten executable-object checks: real NOP sites and compiled-out absence;
    all seven PF decisions, five pressure sites, two eligibility sites, both
    DMU completion sites and both ZFS access completion sites checked.
  Three base libzpool object builds: dmu_tx.o, txg.o and zil.o.
  Three backend guard checks: FreeBSD kernel forwarding, userland no-op,
    and foreign-kernel no-op with no argument evaluation.
  Fourteen offline profile compilations: normal and PID/duration filtered,
    plus exact selector/argument contracts and base installation selection.
  Two additional PF baseline/current compilations with diagnostic __FILE__
    and __LINE__ normalized: their executable .text bytes match. The raw
    comparison retained equal size but differed in diagnostic line constants;
    both results are retained. This limited code-generation check is not a
    runtime correctness or packet-performance benchmark.

PF and ZFS are modules in the prepared kernel's selected object list, so the
new code is validated by module links; no new monolithic kernel link is claimed.
Nothing was loaded, installed, booted or mounted. /dev/dtrace is unavailable,
so live firing, existing PF limiter traffic regressions and ZFS storage/stress
regressions were not run against these modules. No pool state, firewall rules,
network configuration or storage devices were changed.

Remaining gaps include PF limiter administration and broader shaping/NAT64
qualification, ZFS scrub/resilver/trim/import/recovery and device-specific I/O,
and comprehensive hardware/runtime validation. Existing upstream probes in
those areas are not proof that all nine categories are complete. The manifest
and session-only reverse-checked patch preserve the exact reviewed snapshot.

Update 2026-10-15: FreeBSD pf was retired when OpenBSD PF landed.  The PF
contracts above keep their names and arguments and now fire from
contrib/openbsd-pf/sys/net (marked "5BSD: DTrace"); see dtrace_pf(4).
